/*****************************************************************************
    jitJF -- THE STRUCTURE RUNG for Increment 1 (frame model), 2026-08-01.

    ⚠⚠ THIS RUNG IS NOT THE PROOF, AND IT SAYS SO IN ITS OWN HEADER.
    Without recursion, allocas-for-locals is BEHAVIOUR-NEUTRAL: one activation's
    alloca and one field's storage hold the same value at every observable
    point, so the answers are identical either way. A green value here certifies
    NOTHING about the frame model. What it asserts is STRUCTURE plus a
    regression net. THE PROOF IS J-R -- factorial-shaped, fired at TWO depths,
    because depth-1 passes on aliased slots and depth-N cannot.

    ⚠ WHY THIS FIXTURE HAD TO EXIST AT ALL, and it is the finding of the
    increment: EVERY EXISTING LADDER RUNG USES GLOBALS. jitJ1's `jaIn`/`jaOut`
    are declared in the define block, so they are registry fields, not action
    locals -- and Part III says globals KEEP their baked addresses. So the
    prologue correctly did nothing for J1..J7, the ladder stayed 47/47 green,
    and a structure claim read off that would have been VACUOUS. Measured by
    dumping J1's IR after the build: no frame alloca, both operands still
    `inttoptr`. The rung exists because the net could not see the change.

    THE DISCRIMINATOR IS ONE FUNCTION CONTAINING BOTH KINDS:
        jfTmp   -- never declared anywhere; born by assignment INSIDE code={},
                   so aCTionNamE makes it isLocal. MUST get an alloca.
        jfOut   -- declared in the define block, therefore a global.
                   MUST keep its baked inttoptr address and immediate store.
    Asserting only "an alloca exists" would pass if the prologue framed
    everything; asserting only "a baked address exists" would pass if it framed
    nothing. Both, in one function, is what pins the schema.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (CLAUDE.md third corollary).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(JitLadder);
define
    jfIn;
    jfOut;
    jitJF code={ jfTmp = jfIn + 5; jfOut = jfTmp + 100; };
    ;

jfIn  = 10;
jfOut = 0;
print "JF fire 1 setup : jfIn =" jfIn:;
testing(jitJF);
print "JF fire 1 result: jfOut =" jfOut " (want 115)":;

/*  ⚠ NO RECOMPILE BELOW THIS LINE. Only the input changes. Fire-twice is what
    separates "compiled code computed it" from "the interpreter did it at emit
    time and the answer happened to be right".  */
jfIn  = 30;
jfOut = 0;
print "JF fire 2 setup : jfIn =" jfIn " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JF fire 2 result: jfOut =" jfOut " (want 135)":;
/*  ⚠ THE ORACLE'S TESTIMONY, captured LAST and that order is LOAD-BEARING.
    The interpreter is the differential oracle and section 0 sentences it to
    death, so every rung records what it said while it can still say it.
    BUT: calling the action interpreted BEFORE testing() consumes its isCoded
    state, and testing() then silently routes to jitRunIfTest -- the hand-built
    smoke scaffold -- instead of jitRunAction. Measured on J3, 2026-07-31.

    FOR THIS RUNG THE ORACLE CARRIES EXTRA WEIGHT, because the interpreted path
    is the one that still uses saveLocalFields/restoreLocalFields -- the machinery
    this increment begins to replace. Interpreted and jitted agreeing here is the
    schema agreeing with itself across both implementations, which is the whole
    point of INHERITING the predicate rather than inventing a parallel one.  */
jfIn  = 10;
jfOut = 0;
jitJF();
print "JF interpreted  : jfOut =" jfOut " (the oracle -- want 115)":;

print "JF SENTINEL":;
stop();
